items(233,[
I('P000367','definition','The critical exponent of a finite-dimensional norm',r'The notes define an exponent \(q\) by \(\|A\|=\|A^q\|=1\Rightarrow r(A)=1\), together with a counterexample for \(q-1\). The dimension sign is visually ambiguous between finite dimension and \(\le\infty\).',r'For a finite-dimensional real or complex normed space \(E\), let \(q(E)\) be the least positive integer \(q\), if it exists, such that every contraction \(A\) with \(\|A^q\|=1\) has spectral radius one; otherwise set \(q(E)=\infty\). For \(q>1\), minimality gives a contraction \(B\) with \(\|B^{q-1}\|=1\) and \(r(B)<1\). In dimension one, \(q(E)=1\), and the notebook’s additional requirement \(\|B\|=1\) cannot be imposed on a predecessor witness.',r'The definition and equivalent eventual-norm-drop formulation are given in \citet[p.~672]{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical}, referring to earlier work of Pták and Mařík. It predates this September talk.',r'the finite-dimensional invariant is well defined with the stated convention. An infinite-dimensional extension requires care: spectral radius, spectral values, and norm-attainment arguments cannot simply be copied.',reconstruction=r'Finite dimension and the dimension-one exception are explicit editorial qualifications.',refs=P),
I('P000367','theorem','Critical exponent of a Hilbert space',r'The table gives \(q(\ell_2^n)=n\).',r'For a real or complex Hilbert space of finite dimension \(n\), every contraction \(A\) with \(\|A^n\|=1\) has spectral radius one, and the exponent is sharp. For \(n\ge2\), a nilpotent shift has \(\|A^{n-1}\|=1\) and \(A^n=0\); dimension one is immediate.',r'This was Pták’s 1962 result, cited on p.~672 of \citet{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical}. \citet[\S\S1--2]{ptak1976isometric} gives a later proof by isometric parts. The 1976 date is not the date the theorem first became known.',r'the exact exponent is established. The Hilbert geometry is essential; an arbitrary norm on the same vector space may have a different exponent or no finite one.',publication_match='exact',refs=P+['ptak1976isometric']),
I('P000367','theorem','Critical exponents of the one and infinity norms',r'The table gives \(q(\ell_1^n)=q(\ell_\infty^n)=n^2-n+1\).',r'For the usual \(\ell_1\) and \(\ell_\infty\) norms in dimension \(n\), the critical exponent is \(n^2-n+1\), with the predecessor-witness convention separately understood for \(n=1\). Duality identifies the two exponents because \(\|(A^*)^j\|=\|A^j\|\) and \(r(A^*)=r(A)\).',r'\citet[p.~672]{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical} explicitly records these established values and their earlier references. Thus they were not new in September 1971; the historical source for the exact maximum-norm result is earlier work of Mařík and Pták.',r'the exact values are established. They demonstrate why dimension alone does not determine the exponent.',publication_match='exact',refs=P),
I('P000367','claim','A two-dimensional norm with no finite critical exponent',r'The notebook attributes an example to Danzer and sketches a unit ball with rotations by small angles; it asserts \(q(E)=\infty\) in dimension two.',r'There exists a real two-dimensional normed space for which, for every integer \(m\), a contraction \(A_m\) has \(\|A_m^m\|=1\) but \(r(A_m)<1\). A bare rotation has spectral radius one and is not by itself such a witness; the norm geometry and the contracting linear map must be specified.',r'\citet[example, pp.~674--675]{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical} gives an explicit two-dimensional example using a piecewise algebraic unit sphere and diagonal contracting maps. This verifies the existence claim before the talk, but it does not identify the notebook’s Danzer diagram or its attribution.',r'the existence of infinite critical exponent in dimension two is established. The exact Danzer construction remains an unresolved bibliographic detail, not an open existence problem.',publication_match='partial',refs=P,confidence='medium'),
I('P000367','question','Finiteness for finite-dimensional lp spaces',r'The notes say existence of a critical exponent for \(\ell_p^n\) is unknown except for \(p=2^m\), and call it useful for numerical analysis.',r'For each fixed dimension \(n\) and \(1\le p<\infty\), ask whether \(q(\ell_p^n)<\infty\), specifying real or complex scalars. A verified partial answer is affirmative for every rational \(p\): if the unit sphere lies in a real algebraic zero set not containing the origin, a finite exponent exists, and rational-\(p\) spheres satisfy that criterion.',r'\citet[Theorem 1 and Corollaries 2--3]{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical}, received 14 January 1970 and printed May--June 1971, proves rational-\(p\) finiteness over both scalar fields. The notebook’s state-of-knowledge claim is already obsolete on its catalogue date. Searches through November 2026 did not verify an exact all-real-\(p\) successor or optimal general exponents.',r'rational-\(p\) finiteness is established. The status of the remaining irrational parameters and sharp quantitative values was not established by this bounded search; this is not a certification that they are open today.',**{'class':'C'},literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',problem='GP-1971-01',worth=r'A focused follow-up is worthwhile to identify the exact irrational-\(p\) literature and sharpen an explicitly chosen finite-dimensional case; the algebraic proof itself is already published.',refs=P),
I('P000367','question','Does the minimum critical exponent tend to infinity with dimension?',r'The notes define \(M(n)\) as the minimum over norms, report \(M(n)\le n\), examples below \(n\), and a subsequence on the scale \(\sqrt n\), and ask whether \(\liminf M(n)=\infty\).',r'Fix the scalar field and define \(M(n)=\min\{q(E):\dim E=n\}\), using \(q(E)=\infty\) when necessary. The Hilbert norm gives \(M(n)\le n\). The definite question is whether, for every integer \(K\), only finitely many dimensions admit a norm with \(q(E)\le K\). The notebook’s subsequence claim is a separate reported bound whose precise construction was not located.',r'Pták’s Hilbert result verifies the basic upper bound \citep{ptak1976isometric}. Bilingual searches of critical exponents, minimum over norms, and \(M(n)\) did not establish the exact later fate. The 1995 literature on ``exponential numbers'' uses a different orbit-growth invariant and cannot be substituted for this contraction exponent.',r'the historical question is precise, and some special-space bounds are established, but its exact current resolution remains unverified here. Class C records that partial development and unresolved identification, not a proof of present-day openness.',**{'class':'C'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='partial',verification='indirect',problem='GP-1971-02',worth=r'It is worth first recovering the precise norm construction behind the reported \(\sqrt n\) subsequence; without that bibliographic step a new asymptotic claim would risk duplicating or misidentifying existing work.',refs=['ptak1976isometric'],confidence='medium'),
I('P000367','claim','The twelve-dimensional direct-sum example is not securely reconstructible',r'The scan writes a sum rendered \(\ell_4^2\oplus_{\ell_1}\ell_8^2\), followed by dimension 12 and critical exponent 11.',r'With the conventional notation \(\ell_p^n\), the displayed sum has dimension four, not twelve. Swapping the roles of superscript and subscript would give Hilbert spaces of dimensions four and eight, but this reading is not established by the scan and its exponent must not be inferred. A unique normed space and claimed exact exponent cannot presently be assigned.',r'The direct-sum construction was not located securely in the searched Pták literature. The preceding discussion of \(M(n)<n\) makes it plausible that a twelve-dimensional example was intended, but plausibility does not resolve the notation.',r'the example remains unidentified. Neither the exponent 11 for the literal four-dimensional space nor a proposed repaired twelve-dimensional space is claimed verified.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='annotated',confidence='low'),
I('P000367','problem','The constrained power-norm extremum',r'The page defines \(C(E,r,\rho)=\max\{\|T^r\|:\|T\|\le1,\ r(T)\le\rho\}\), with \(0<\rho<1\).',r'For finite-dimensional \(E\), integer \(r\ge1\), and \(0\le\rho<1\), the admissible set is compact, so this maximum exists. If \(q(E)<\infty\) and \(r\ge q(E)\), then \(C(E,r,\rho)<1\) for every fixed \(\rho<1\). Conversely, if \(r<q(E)\), some \(\rho<1\) gives \(C(E,r,\rho)=1\). Determining exact values and maximizers is a quantitative problem depending on the norm, dimension, \(r\), and \(\rho\).',r'The extremal viewpoint is part of Pták’s programme; the critical-exponent results in \citet{ptak1976isometric} concern its qualitative threshold. The compactness implication stated here is an elementary editorial deduction, not a claim that the full optimization has been solved. Searches did not identify a uniform exact solution for arbitrary norms.',r'the qualitative finite-dimensional relation to \(q(E)\) is clear. Exact general values and the complete later classification of extremizers were not verified in this run; no blanket open-problem claim is made.',**{'class':'C'},publication_match='partial',problem='GP-1971-03',worth=r'For a specified low-dimensional norm and fixed spectral-radius bound, a certified optimization or sharp estimate could be a bounded contribution; the unrestricted problem is too broad for a defensible new result here.',reconstruction=r'Compactness, continuity of spectral radius, and attainment justify the maximum and strict inequality; these hypotheses and deductions are supplied explicitly.',refs=['ptak1976isometric'],confidence='medium')
])
problems([
{'id':'GP-1971-01','title':'Critical exponents of finite-dimensional lp norms','statement':r'For fixed \(n\) and \(1\le p<\infty\), over the real and complex scalar fields separately, does every norm-one contraction on \(\ell_p^n\) that preserves norm through a bounded number of powers have spectral radius one? Equivalently, is \(q(\ell_p^n)<\infty\)?','occurrences':['1971-233.5'],'status':r'As of November 2026, the verified result is finiteness for every rational \(p\), already proved before the talk by \citet[Theorem 1, Corollaries 2--3]{kirzhnertabachnikov1971critical}. The exact later fate for irrational \(p\) and optimal bounds was not established in this search; absence of a verified successor is not asserted to mean current openness.','status_date':'2026-11','refs':P},
{'id':'GP-1971-02','title':'Growth of the minimum critical exponent over all norms','statement':r'For a fixed scalar field let \(M(n)=\min_{\dim E=n}q(E)\). Must \(\liminf_{n\to\infty}M(n)=\infty\)?','occurrences':['1971-233.6'],'status':r'As of November 2026, the Hilbert bound \(M(n)\le n\) is verified, but neither the notebook’s claimed square-root subsequence construction nor the precise later resolution of the lower-growth question was identified. The similarly named 1995 exponential-number invariant does not answer this question.','status_date':'2026-11','refs':['ptak1976isometric']},
{'id':'GP-1971-03','title':'Maximizing a power norm under a spectral-radius constraint','statement':r'For a specified finite-dimensional normed space \(E\), determine \(C(E,r,\rho)=\max\{\|T^r\|:\|T\|\le1,\ r(T)\le\rho\}\), including sharp bounds and extremizers, for \(r\ge1\), \(0\le\rho<1\).','occurrences':['1971-233.8'],'status':r'As of November 2026, compactness gives existence of the maximum and the critical exponent characterizes when it is strictly below one for every fixed \(\rho<1\). This does not evaluate the maximum. The exact state of the optimization problem for arbitrary norms was not verified; specific Pták operator results must be matched separately.','status_date':'2026-11','refs':['ptak1976isometric']}
])
S=['horrocks1957schubert','tamvakis2004connection']
items(239,[
I('P000373-P000374','construction','Schubert cells in a complex Grassmannian',r'The notes choose \(a_1\le\cdots\le a_n\le N\), a complete flag \(E_j\), and jumps \(a_i+i\) for \(\dim(F\cap E_j)\).',r'For \(\operatorname{Gr}(n,\mathbb C^N)\), choose \(0\le a_1\le\cdots\le a_n\le N-n\). The corresponding Schubert cell consists of planes whose intersection-dimension function jumps precisely at \(b_i=a_i+i\); it is an affine space of complex dimension \(\sum_i a_i\). The closure is defined by the associated rank inequalities and is a projective Schubert variety.',r'The notebook explicitly cites \citet{horrocks1957schubert}, published in 1957. The geometry and its indexing are also checked in \citet[\S4]{tamvakis2004connection}; his quotient-bundle convention is transposed from the usual subspace convention and should not be copied without translation.',r'the affine decomposition is established. Requiring equality only at a few selected flag dimensions, without the complete jump data, does not specify the open cell.',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=S),
I('P000374','theorem','Schubert cycles give an integral homology basis',r'The cell decomposition is used to compare bases in homology and cohomology.',r'The closures of the complex Schubert cells give a \(\mathbb Z\)-basis of the homology of the Grassmannian. The affine cells have even real dimensions, so the cellular boundary maps vanish and odd homology is zero. A cohomology basis is obtained by Poincaré duality, with complementary rather than equal complex dimensions.',r'This is classical Schubert calculus underlying \citet{horrocks1957schubert}; \citet[\S4]{tamvakis2004connection} makes the cycle/cohomology conventions explicit. The seminar is expository.',r'the integral assertion is established, stronger than a real de Rham basis. Open noncompact cells themselves are not ordinary compact integration cycles.',refs=S),
I('P000374','method','Basic invariant forms on the homogeneous quotient',r'The Grassmannian is \(U(N)/(U(n)\times U(N-n))\). Forms are lifted to \(U(N)\); the first descent condition is written as vanishing when the listed arguments lie in the subgroup tangent space.',r'For a principal \(H\)-bundle \(G\to G/H\), a differential form descends precisely when it is \(H\)-invariant and horizontal: insertion of any one vertical tangent vector gives zero. Vanishing only when all arguments are vertical is insufficient in degree greater than one. Invariant forms on the Grassmannian are the \(H\)-invariant alternating tensors on \(\mathfrak g/\mathfrak h\).',r'This is the basic-form construction behind the Chern--Weil treatment in \citet[\S3]{tamvakis2004connection}, tracing the classical work of Chern, Weil, and Cartan. The descent criterion is older than the talk.',r'the criterion is established. The notebook’s compressed first condition must be read in the stronger insertion sense for its later construction to work.',literal_correctness='false as written',refs=['tamvakis2004connection']),
I('P000374','construction','The curvature matrix and invariant characteristic forms',r'The Maurer--Cartan entries \(\theta_{jk}\) are used in \(\Theta_{jk}=-(2\pi i)^{-1}\sum_s\theta_{js}\wedge\overline{\Theta}_{ks}\); the second factor is marked graphically uncertain. Conjugation-invariant polynomials in this matrix descend to the Grassmannian.',r'For the tautological Hermitian bundle or its dual, the induced connection has a curvature matrix whose entries are sums of wedges of two off-diagonal Maurer--Cartan one-forms. In a compatible convention the displayed second factor must be \(\overline\theta_{ks}\), so the result has degree two. A degree-\(r\) invariant polynomial in normalized curvature is a closed \(2r\)-form with connection-independent cohomology class.',r'This is classical Chern--Weil theory, explicitly reviewed in \citet[\S3 and \S4]{tamvakis2004connection}. The scan does not conclusively distinguish upper- and lower-case theta in the uncertain factor; degree counting gives the mathematical emendation, not paleographic certainty.',r'the curvature construction is established. The sign depends on whether the tautological bundle, quotient, or dual is used; that choice must be made before identifying its Chern or Schur class.',reconstruction=r'The lower-case second factor is a mathematical reconstruction. The case uncertainty is preserved in the corrections rather than called a proven transcription error.',refs=['tamvakis2004connection']),
I('P000374-P000375','theorem','The alternant quotient as a Schur polynomial and character',r'A quotient of determinants with exponents \(\lambda_n,\lambda_{n-1}+1,\ldots,\lambda_1+n-1\) is said to be a polynomial and the character of the representation with highest weight \(\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_n\ge0\).',r'For a partition \(\lambda\) of length at most \(n\), \(s_\lambda(x)=\det(x_i^{\lambda_j+n-j})/\det(x_i^{n-j})\) extends to a symmetric polynomial, including repeated eigenvalues. It is the character of the irreducible polynomial representation of \(GL_n\) with highest weight \(\lambda\). Substituting a matrix of commuting even-degree forms is interpreted through the polynomial, not by dividing differential forms.',r'The representation-theoretic identity is classical and is the algebraic input of \citet{horrocks1957schubert}. \citet[\S2]{tamvakis2004connection} verifies the Schur functor and invariant-polynomial interpretation directly.',r'the formula is established. The notebook’s reversed order of determinant columns gives the same quotient because numerator and denominator acquire the same sign.',literal_correctness='correct',refs=S),
I('P000375','claim','Schur curvature forms and the dual Schubert basis',r'The notes substitute curvature into the Schur polynomial, put \(a_i=N-n-\lambda_i\), and assert integral one over the corresponding Schubert cell and zero over the others; form degree is equated with cell dimension.',r'In the standard subspace convention, let \(S\) be the rank-\(n\) tautological bundle and let \(\lambda\) fit the \(n\times(N-n)\) rectangle. The Schur class \(s_\lambda(S^*)\) is the Schubert cohomology class \(\sigma_\lambda\) of degree \(2|\lambda|\). If \(\lambda^\vee_i=N-n-\lambda_{n+1-i}\), then \(\int_{[\Omega_\mu]}\sigma_\lambda=\delta_{\mu,\lambda^\vee}\) for \(|\mu|=n(N-n)-|\lambda|\). Integrate over closed Schubert cycles, with consistent indexing and real degree.',r'This is the Schur-form/Schubert-class relation of \citet{horrocks1957schubert}; a complete modern formulation is \citet[Theorem 1, \S4]{tamvakis2004connection}. The notebook’s displayed indexing conflates the cycle Poincaré-dual to a class with the cycle on which that class evaluates to one.',r'the corrected duality statement is established. The literal formula is generally dimensionally inconsistent unless the partition and cycle are complemented appropriately.',literal_correctness='false as written',reconstruction=r'The dual bundle and standard partition convention are fixed here explicitly; the notebook’s uncertain \(N^{\prime}\) is not interpreted as a new ambient dimension.',refs=S),
I('P000375','problem','Gelfand’s unspecified request to describe a modular group algebra',r'I. M. Gelfand asks to describe the group ring of \(SL_2(\mathbb F_q)\) with coefficients in the algebraic closure of \(\mathbb F_q\).',r'The object is \(\overline{\mathbb F}_q[SL_2(\mathbb F_q)]\), in the defining characteristic. The word ``describe'' does not specify whether one seeks simple modules, blocks, the Jacobson radical, a presentation, indecomposables, or a Morita-equivalent algebra. These are different questions. Since the characteristic divides the group order, a semisimple character decomposition alone does not describe this algebra.',r'The object is identifiable but the requested invariant is not. Searches of modular \(SL_2\) group-algebra descriptions did not turn this wording into one uniquely matchable problem, so no theorem about simple representations is substituted for a full algebra classification.',r'this occurrence remains an underdetermined request, not a certified unsolved problem. It receives no new problem-index identifier because no definite target is stated.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
])
